This patch, formerly the Island patch, adds these things to the game:

- New "island" rooms, which have a moat of water or lava, a tree and
buried treasure.
- Underground "rivers" occaisionally meandering through levels.
- A removal of the restriction that only one kind of special room can
appear on a dungeon level

In this version of the patch, the additions are better formatted, some
bugs are fixed, rivers now cause the formation of boulders (loosened
by the workings of erosion) that can often be pushed into the water
(which is fun), some initial monsters are now generated in the river
depending on depth, and secret doors on island rooms are made
unhidden.  Other assorted tweaking has also been done.

This version of the patch can be found at:
http://www.hiddenglade.com/download/terrain1-2.diff

Left to do: come up with a solution to remaining cases of player
trapping.


And now, some spoilers:









How do you get across the moat, be it water or lava, to the island? 
Note that I didn't add any of these things: they're all already
included in vanilla Nethack.

Water:
- Push a boulder into the water and hope it doesn't sink.  While the
water on an island is a moat in function, it's actually of type POOL,
so it's much easier to bridge than a "real" moat.  Scrolls of earth,
some of which are guarenteed in Sokoban, can help out here.
- Zap a wand of fire, or other source of fire.  This leaves pits, and
sometimes the adjacent water will flow into them leaving you where you
started.
- Zap with a wand of cold, or other source of cold.  This freezes the
water over, though it may be possible to break through the ice.  (?)
- Jump, with boots of jumping or as a knight.
- Teleport, preferably with control.  Also, try falling down a trap
door from the floor above repeatedly and hope to land inside the room.
 An additional means of teleport is needed to get off the island
(though there is always a trap door on the island to facilitate
escape).
- Boots (or some other means) of levitation, or boots of
water-walking.
- Polymorph into something amphibious, or that levitates.  You can
discover what these are by finding an island and seeing which monsters
will cross the moat.
- Jump in the water, and hope you emerge from the opposite shore. 
It's best to drop items capable of water damage first, or throw them
over.

Note that both floating eyes and news are already able to cross water.
 No protection here....

Lava is much harder to cross.  Means of teleport, levitation,
polymorph or jumping will get you by.  Sources of cold will solidify
the lava.  Please, please, please do not try to jump into the lava
unless you're suitably polymorphed.  This is a textbook example of a
bad idea.


Rivers now have a selection of sea monsters in them.  There is the
potential of encountering monsters that could be considered a little
out of depth, especailly jellyfish (who can poison) and piranha. 
Taking advantage of the fact that they can't come up onto land, and
that sea monsters are often slow relative to land monsters, will help
out here.

